 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Types",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "Standard and high-capacity (-EU) types share the coil and the footprint. The high-capacity type is rated 10 A on the NO contact at 250 VAC instead of 125 VAC and lasts 25,000 operations at that load, but its ambient range ends at 85°C rather than 105°C and its coil may be overdriven less at high temperature. Sealed versions (4 in the code) are RT III; flux-protected ones are RT II."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Maximum coil voltage",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "The coil tolerates up to 190% of its rated voltage from -40°C to 23°C. Above 23°C the limit falls in a straight line to about 130% at 105°C (standard) or about 145% at 85°C (high-capacity). This limit applies to the top of a fluctuating supply range, not to a voltage held on the coil continuously."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Reduced holding voltage",
   "page": 6,
   "text": "Coil power can be cut once the relay has pulled in. Give the coil its rated voltage for 100 ms or longer, then lower it to the holding voltage: at least 40% of rated for G5Q-1A types and at least 30% for G5Q-1 types. Supply variation must never take the coil below that level. Holding brings coil power to about 32 mW (1a) or 36 mW (1c)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "PWM holding (-PW models)",
   "page": 6,
   "text": "Models with the -PW suffix may be held with a pulse-width-modulated coil current instead of a lowered DC level. Pull-in still needs 100 ms or more at full rated voltage before pulsing starts. Omron validated PWM holding with the rated voltage as pulse amplitude, a duty of 50% or more, a frequency of 10 kHz or more and a freewheel diode with a forward voltage of 0.4 V or less."
  }
 ],
